ies that boast a larger  customer account are consumer Brands  like Nestle and Coca-Cola or someone  like Johnson and Johnson but those  aren't tech companies so they don't  exactly have a bunch of data on you like  Oracle a couple of years ago Larry  Ellison even made a presentation in  which he made his intentions of  controlling the entire population at  blatantly obvious he said quote how many  people are on Earth 7 billion 2 billion  to go on my last video about sap a lot  of people commented that sap isn't  actually all that powerful given that  they simply sell software to companies  but like I said in that video that's  because sap doesn't do anything  nefarious with it even though the they  could and Oracle is a perfect example of  just what they could do just a few  months ago a class action lawsuit was  brought against Oracle for allegedly  collecting profile and selling the data  of 5 billion users so here's the story  of how Oracle became the most discreet  but also the most powerful giant within  the entire tech industry  taking a look back the story of Oracle  dates back to 1977 to three men named  Baba Miner at oats and Larry Ellison the  company started off as a database  software developer in fact the company's  original name was actually software  development Laboratories or sdl the  inspiration for the company was from a  research paper written by Edgar F Cod  that described the intricacies of  relational database Management systems  at first Alison's goal was to create a  piece of software that was compatible  for IBM's system or database but unlike  Xerox ibmo wasn't absolute idiot who  just handed out their intellectual  property like candy so the trio would  have an extremely difficult time at  trying to troubleshoot IBM error codes  and it would eventually shift their  focus onto an easier Target the  government the US government has this  policy of giving everyone an equal  chance of trying to land a government  contracts they post their co